      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Published by the BMJ Publishing Group Limited. For permission to use (where not already granted under a licence) please go to http://www.bmj.com/company/products-services/rights-and-licensing/",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To determine optimum measurements for abdominal obesity and to assess the prevalence and determinants of metabolic syndrome in Qatar."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "National health survey."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Qatar National STEPwise Survey conducted by the Supreme Council of Health during 2012."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "2496 Qatari citizens aged 18-64 representative of the general population."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PRIMARY AND SECONDARY OUTCOME MEASURES",
            "content": "Measure of obesity (body mass index, waist circumference or waist-to-height ratio) that best identified the presence of at least 2 other factors of metabolic syndrome; cut-off values of waist circumference; frequency of metabolic syndrome."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Waist circumference ≥102 for men and ≥94\u2005cm for women was the best predictor of the presence of other determinants of metabolic syndrome (raised blood pressure, fasting blood glucose, triglycerides and reduced high-density lipoprotein cholesterol). Using these values, we identified 28% of Qataris with metabolic syndrome, which is considerably lower than the estimate of 37% calculated using the International Diabetes Federation (IDF) criteria. Restricting the analysis to participants without known elevated blood pressure, elevated blood sugar or diabetes 16.5% would be classified as having metabolic syndrome. In a multivariable logistic regression analysis, the prevalence of metabolic syndrome increased steadily with age (OR=3.40 (95% CI 2.02 to 5.74), OR=5.66 (3.65 to 8.78), OR=10.2 (5.98 to 17.6) and OR=18.2 (7.01 to 47.5) for those in the age group '30-39', '40-49', '50-59', '60-64' vs '18-29'; p<0.0001), decreased with increasing educational attainment (OR=0.61 (0.39 to 0.96) for those who attained 'secondary school or more' compared with 'less than primary school'; p=0.03) and exercise (OR=0.60 (0.42 to 0.86) for those exercising ≥3000 vs <600 MET-min/week; p=0.006) but was not associated with smoking or diet."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Waist circumference was the best measure of obesity to combine with other variables to construct a country-specific definition of metabolic syndrome in Qatar. Approximately 28% of adult Qatari citizens satisfy the criteria for metabolic syndrome, which increased significantly with age. Education and physical activity were inversely associated with this syndrome."
          }
        ]
      },
